Orchestrator: Routing

Purpose:
  Define agent selection, capability matching, policy evaluation,
  routing logic, handoff contracts, and trust model for the SDDRA
  execution engine.

Owns: ./

Scope:
  Agent definition and identity
  Capability registry and matching
  Agent selection scoring
  Policy evaluation for execution
  Cost-aware and context-aware routing
  Agent handoff contracts
  Agent affinity and reputation
  Trust levels and autonomy levels
  Centralized vs decentralized orchestration
  Explainable routing

Principles:
  Agent = WHO executes.
  Skill = HOW work is done.
  Task = WHAT must be done.
  Tool = WITH WHAT capability.
  Policy = WHETHER allowed.
  Agent is execution participant, not source of truth.
  SDD is source of truth.

Agent:
  definition:
    autonomous execution unit with capabilities, skills, and policies

  structure:
    id: string (e.g. AGENT-BACKEND-001)
    role: string (e.g. backend-developer)
    capabilities: [CAP-GO, CAP-POSTGRES, CAP-REST]
    skills: [SKILL-API-IMPLEMENT, SKILL-UNIT-TEST]
    tools: [read, write, test]
    autonomy: integer (0..4)
    trust: string (TRUST-LOW, TRUST-MEDIUM, TRUST-HIGH)
    model: string (optional LLM model binding)

  rule: agent identity is separate from model
  rule: agent can bind to multiple models

Capability:
  definition:
    concrete ability to perform specific work

  examples:
    - GO
    - POSTGRESQL
    - REST
    - KUBERNETES
    - SECURITY-REVIEW

  registry:
    CAP-GO
    CAP-POSTGRES
    CAP-KAFKA
    CAP-KUBERNETES
    CAP-SECURITY

CapabilityMatching:
  formula:
    score = required_capabilities_matched / total_required_capabilities

  rule: 100% match required for mandatory capabilities
  rule: extra capabilities are allowed but not required

SkillMatching:
  rule: agent MUST support required skill version
  rule: version incompatibility MUST block assignment

AgentSelectionScore:
  components:
    capability_match: weight_high
    skill_match: weight_high
    availability: weight_medium
    quality: weight_medium
    cost: weight_low
    risk_fit: weight_high
    context_fit: weight_medium

  rule: weights are project-configurable
  rule: highest scoring eligible agent is selected

Availability:
  states:
    - AVAILABLE
    - BUSY
    - OFFLINE
    - DISABLED

  rule: only AVAILABLE agents are considered for assignment

AgentPool:
  rule: scheduler selects from eligible pool
  rule: load balancing distributes work evenly
  rule: domain affinity prefers context-familiar agents

AgentAffinity:
  definition:
    preference for agent with existing context in specific domain

  example:
    PAYMENT domain tasks prefer AGENT-PAYMENT-01
    because context familiarity reduces token cost

ContextCost:
  definition:
    token cost to load full context for a task

  rule: context cost is a routing criterion
  rule: familiar agent may have lower context cost

CostAwareRouting:
  rule: low-risk tasks MAY use cheaper models
  rule: high-risk tasks MUST use high-quality models
  rule: model selection is abstracted from agent identity

ModelRouting:
  mapping:
    Simple CRUD -> fast model
    Architecture decision -> reasoning model
    Security review -> high-quality model

  rule: agent abstraction hides model selection

AgentPolicy:
  definition:
    agent-level execution restrictions

  example:
    allowed: [development, testing]
    forbidden: [production]

TaskPolicy:
  definition:
    task-level execution requirements

  example:
    required_approval: [security-team]

FullAuthorization:
  chain:
    Task
      ↓
    Agent Capability Check
      ↓
    Skill Permission Check
      ↓
    Task Policy
      ↓
    Agent Policy
      ↓
    Environment Policy
      ↓
    ALLOW / DENY / REQUIRE_APPROVAL

  rule: all checks MUST pass before execution

CapabilityVsPermission:
  capability:
    definition: can do (technical ability)
    example: CAN use PostgreSQL

  permission:
    definition: may do (authorization)
    example: CAN modify production database

  rule: capability does not imply permission
  rule: permission is enforced by policy engine

AgentHandoff:
  contract:
    task_id: string
    current_state: string
    completed_work: artifact
    pending_work: [items]
    artifacts: [outputs]
    failures: [incidents]
    decisions: [decisions]
    context: minimal_relevant_context

  rule: handoff MUST include all required context
  rule: receiving agent MUST NOT read full reasoning history

AgentChain:
  example:
    ARCHITECT -> CODER -> QA -> SECURITY -> DEVOPS

  rule: chain order is deterministic
  rule: handoff artifacts are the only communication channel

AgentParallelism:
  example:
            CODER
           /     \
          ↓       ↓
        QA       SECURITY
           \     /
            ↓
          REVIEW

  rule: parallel agents MUST have synchronization points

Supervisor:
  responsibilities:
    - task decomposition
    - agent selection
    - result validation
    - handoff orchestration
    - failure recovery

  rule: supervisor is centralized orchestrator
  rule: agents do NOT negotiate directly with each other

CentralizedOrchestration:
  architecture:
    SDD -> Orchestrator -> Agents

  advantages:
    - control
    - observability
    - deterministic workflow

  disadvantage:
    - bottleneck potential

DecentralizedOrchestration:
  architecture:
    Agent <-> Agent <-> Agent

  disadvantages:
    - unpredictable
    - harder to debug
    - harder governance

  rule: SDD uses centralized orchestration with controlled collaboration

AgentProposal:
  protocol:
    1. Agent detects need for additional work
    2. Agent creates SECURITY-REVIEW-REQUEST (or similar)
    3. Orchestrator validates request against SDD graph
    4. Orchestrator creates task and routes agent

  rule: agent proposals are not direct execution

AgentAutonomyLevels:
  L0: observe
  L1: suggest
  L2: execute approved task
  L3: autonomous execution
  L4: autonomous orchestration

  rule: L4 is ONLY allowed within explicit trust boundaries
  rule: default is L2

TrustLevels:
  TRUST-LOW:
    scope: read-only
    environment: development

  TRUST-MEDIUM:
    scope: development
    environment: development, testing

  TRUST-HIGH:
    scope: production
    environment: any
    rule: still requires policy/approval for critical actions

AgentReputation:
  metrics:
    - success_rate
    - average_duration
    - test_failure_rate
    - review_score
    - cost_per_task

  rule: reputation is advisory, not deterministic
  rule: reputation bias MUST be balanced with exploration

ExplainableRouting:
  requirement:
    every agent assignment MUST record reason

  example:
    GO capability = match
    REST capability = match
    skill = match
    availability = available
    risk = compatible
    cost = acceptable

  rule: "Why was this agent selected?" MUST be answerable

AgentFailure:
  classification:
    - agent failure (agent can be retried or replaced)
    - task failure (acceptance criteria not met)

  rule: agent failure does not imply task failure
  rule: task failure requires remediation, not just retry

FailureRouting:
  chain:
    CODER-A -> FAILED -> CODER-B -> FAILED -> SENIOR-CODER -> HUMAN

  rule: escalation chain is deterministic
  rule: human escalation is REQUIRED after exhaustion

AgentContextIsolation:
  rule: agent sees only relevant subgraph
  rule: token budget is enforced
  rule: security-sensitive nodes are never exposed to low-trust agents

MultiAgent:
  definition:
    coordinated execution by multiple specialized agents

  principle:
    Multi-agent != multiple LLM calls
    Multi-agent = Role + Scope + Context + Permission + Task + Communication + Ownership

  why:
    single agent for complex task creates too much responsibility
    specialized agents are more controllable and observable

AgentRole:
  definition:
    functional classification of agent

  examples:
    - ARCHITECT
    - CODER
    - QA
    - SECURITY
    - REVIEWER
    - DEVOPS
    - DOCUMENTATION

  rule: every agent MUST have exactly one primary role
  rule: GENERAL_AGENT is forbidden

AgentResponsibility:
  structure:
    responsibility:
      implement application code

    not_responsible_for:
      - production deployment
      - security approval
      - architecture approval

  rule: responsibility is explicit, not implicit

AgentScope:
  definition:
    boundaries of where agent can work

  example:
    scope:
      project: payments
      modules:
        - refund
        - transaction

  rule: agent cannot modify resources outside scope

AgentPermission:
  definition:
    specific actions agent can perform

  example:
    CODER:
      CAN: READ_FILE, WRITE_FILE, RUN_TEST
      CANNOT: DEPLOY_PRODUCTION

  rule: scope and permission are separate concepts

AgentOwnership:
  definition:
    task graph node ownership

  example:
    TASK-100 owner = ARCHITECT
    TASK-101 owner = CODER
    TASK-102 owner = QA

  rule: every task has exactly one owner

Delegation:
  definition:
    agent assigns work to another agent

  example:
    ARCHITECT -> delegate -> CODER

  rule: delegation MUST follow formal contract

DelegationContract:
  structure:
    delegation:
      from: ARCHITECT
      to: CODER
      task: TASK-101
      input:
        - architecture_spec
        - api_contract
      expected_output:
        - implementation
        - tests

ContextPackage:
  definition:
    minimal context bundle for handoff

  example:
    CONTEXT-PACKAGE-20:
      task: TASK-101
      required_files: [payment.go, refund.go]
      decisions: [ADR-20]
      constraints: [idempotency_required]
      artifacts: [API-SPEC-10]

  rule: handoff does NOT transfer full context
  rule: receiving agent requests additional context if needed

SharedContext:
  definition:
    context available to all agents in workflow

  examples:
    - requirements
    - architecture
    - decisions
    - contracts
    - task graph

PrivateContext:
  definition:
    agent-specific context not shared with others

  examples:
    CODER: implementation details
    SECURITY: security analysis
    QA: test strategy

  rule: private context reduces noise and cost
  rule: minimum required context principle applies

Orchestrator:
  definition:
    component that plans, delegates, schedules, monitors, and coordinates agents

  responsibilities:
    - plan
    - delegate
    - schedule
    - monitor
    - retry
    - coordinate
    - aggregate

  rule: orchestrator is NOT necessarily an LLM agent
  rule: orchestrator workflow is deterministic

SupervisorPattern:
  architecture:
              SUPERVISOR
             /     |     \
            /      |      \
        CODER      QA    SECURITY

  responsibilities:
    - assign
    - review
    - retry
    - escalate

WorkerPattern:
  architecture:
    SUPERVISOR
        │
     ┌──┼────┐
     ▼  ▼    ▼
    W1  W2   W3

  rule: worker only solves assigned task

PipelinePattern:
  architecture:
    ARCHITECT -> CODER -> QA -> SECURITY -> REVIEWER

  rule: agents work sequentially
  rule: handoff artifacts are the only communication

ParallelPattern:
  architecture:
           TASK
            │
      ┌─────┼─────┐
      ▼     ▼     ▼
     QA  SECURITY  DOCS
      │     │       │
      └─────┼───────┘
            ▼
          REVIEW

  rule: parallel agents MUST have synchronization points
  rule: parallel execution only when no blocking dependencies

AgentCommunication:
  rule: agents do NOT negotiate directly with each other
  rule: all communication goes through orchestrator

  flow:
    Agent A -> Message Contract -> Orchestrator -> Agent B

MessageContract:
  structure:
    message:
      type: HANDOFF | TASK_ASSIGNMENT | RESULT | REVIEW_REQUEST | APPROVAL_REQUEST | BLOCKER | QUESTION | ESCALATION
      task_id: string
      artifacts: [items]
      decisions: [items]
      constraints: [items]

AgentResultContract:
  structure:
    result:
      status: SUCCESS | FAILURE | BLOCKED
      artifacts: [ART-100]
      changes: [payment.go]
      tests: [PASS]
      blockers: []
      recommendations: []

  rule: "Done" is not sufficient
  rule: structured result is required

AgentIsolation:
  rule: agents MUST be isolated from each other's partial work

  workspace_isolation:
    example:
      TASK-101 -> WORKSPACE-101 -> CODER

  git_branch_isolation:
    example:
      main
        ├── sdd/task-101
        ├── sdd/task-102
        └── sdd/task-103

ReviewerAgent:
  responsibilities:
    - correctness
    - architecture
    - security
    - style
    - tests
    - requirements

  rule: reviewer is independent from coder
  rule: reviewer does not share coder's full context (confirmation bias prevention)

TwoAgentVerification:
  definition:
    independent validation by second agent

  example:
    CODER -> INDEPENDENT REVIEWER

  rule: critical tasks require two-agent verification
  rule: reviewer runs independent tests, not just reviews code

SecurityGate:
  definition:
    mandatory security check for critical resources

  example:
    CODER -> QA -> SECURITY -> APPROVAL

  resources:
    - production
    - payment
    - authentication
    - PII

ConflictResolution:
  resolution_methods:
    - priority
    - policy
    - decision record
    - human approval
    - reviewer
    - consensus

  rule: security policy > agent preference

Veto:
  definition:
    single agent can block workflow

  example:
    SECURITY: CRITICAL_VIOLATION -> BLOCK

  rule: veto cannot be overridden by majority vote
  rule: veto requires explicit policy justification

AgentEscalation:
  chain:
    CODER -> SENIOR-CODER -> HUMAN

  triggers:
    - insufficient_context
    - permission_denied
    - ambiguous_requirement
    - repeated_failure
    - security_risk
    - budget_exceeded
    - dependency_blocked

  rule: retry != escalation
  rule: human escalation is REQUIRED after exhaustion

PartialProgress:
  definition:
    multi-agent workflow where some agents succeed and others fail

  example:
    Agent A ✓
    Agent B ✓
    Agent C ✗
    Agent D ✓

  rule: partial progress does not automatically mean workflow FAILED
  rule: task graph determines recovery path

AgentQuota:
  structure:
    max_tokens: integer
    max_tool_calls: integer
    max_duration: string
    max_cost: float
    max_parallel_tasks: integer

  rule: quotas prevent runaway agents

AgentScheduling:
  responsibilities:
    - which agent
    - when
    - how many
    - parallel or sequential

  rule: scheduling is deterministic based on task graph

AgentPool:
  definition:
    pool of interchangeable agents with same role

  example:
    CODER POOL:
      - Coder-1
      - Coder-2
      - Coder-3

  rule: available worker is assigned to task

AgentAffinity:
  definition:
    preference for same agent to continue related tasks

  example:
    TASK-1 -> CODER-2
    TASK-2 -> CODER-2 (context continuity)

  rule: affinity is trade-off between cost and reliability

AgentMemory:
  task_memory:
    definition: current execution context
    scope: current task only

  project_memory:
    definition: stable project knowledge
    scope: entire project

  rule: shared memory MUST be controlled, versioned, audited
  rule: shared writable memory causes chaos

AgentCommunicationGraph:
  architecture:
                     ORCHESTRATOR
                          │
         ┌────────────────┼────────────────┐
         ▼                ▼                ▼
     ARCHITECT          CODER             QA
         │                │                │
         └────────────────┼────────────────┘
                          ▼
                       REVIEWER
                          │
                          ▼
                         GATE

  rule: agents primarily communicate through orchestrator
  rule: direct agent communication is forbidden

AgentAuthorityModel:
  principle:
    Agent = intelligence within boundaries
    Authority = comes from SDD governance layer

  model:
    LLM -> Intent -> Agent -> Permission -> Policy -> Gate -> Execution

  rule: agent does not create its own authority
  rule: agent can decide, propose, execute within scope
  rule: agent CANNOT override policy, change ownership, bypass gate, grant permission

MultiAgentPrinciple:
  rule: agents can have autonomy, but NOT authority
  rule: autonomy = decide, propose, execute within scope
  rule: authority = override policy, change ownership, bypass gate, grant permission

Navigation:
  Orchestrator: @../orchestrator/INDEX.sdd
  Pipeline: @../orchestrator/pipeline.sdd
  Runtime: @../runtime/INDEX.sdd
  Execution: @../runtime/execution.sdd
  Context: @../runtime/context.sdd
  Skills: @../skills/INDEX.sdd
  Protocol: @../protocol/INDEX.sdd
  Graph: @../graph/INDEX.sdd
  Tasks: @../tasks/INDEX.sdd
  Workflow: @../workflow/INDEX.sdd
  Observability: @../observability/INDEX.sdd

Rules:
  [RT-MULTI-01] Multi-agent != multiple LLM calls — requires Role, Scope, Context, Permission, Task, Communication, Ownership.
  [RT-MULTI-02] Every agent MUST have exactly one primary role.
  [RT-MULTI-03] Agent responsibility is explicit, not implicit.
  [RT-MULTI-04] Agent scope and permission are separate concepts.
  [RT-MULTI-05] Every task has exactly one owner.
  [RT-MULTI-06] Delegation MUST follow formal contract.
  [RT-MULTI-07] Handoff transfers context package, not full context.
  [RT-MULTI-08] Orchestrator is NOT necessarily an LLM agent — workflow is deterministic.
  [RT-MULTI-09] Agents do NOT negotiate directly — all communication goes through orchestrator.
  [RT-MULTI-10] Parallel agents MUST have synchronization points.
  [RT-MULTI-11] Agent isolation prevents partial work interference.
  [RT-MULTI-12] Reviewer is independent from coder — confirmation bias prevention.
  [RT-MULTI-13] Critical tasks require two-agent verification.
  [RT-MULTI-14] Security veto cannot be overridden by majority vote.
  [RT-MULTI-15] Agents have autonomy but NOT authority.
  [RT-MULTI-16] Authority comes from SDD governance, not agent self-declaration.
  [RT-MULTI-17] Partial progress does not automatically mean workflow FAILED.
  [RT-MULTI-18] Agent memory MUST be controlled, versioned, audited.
  [RT-MULTI-19] Agent quotas prevent runaway agents.
  [RT-MULTI-20] Multi-agent failure does not stop entire system — dependency graph determines impact.
  [RT-MULTI-21] Agent MUST NOT add signatures or sign-offs to any output.
  [RT-MULTI-22] Agent MUST NOT use emojis, icons, or decorative symbols in headers, titles, or messages — reserved for human use only.

State: +
